    {
        "content": "<p>For now this continues to work with <code>set_option backward.do.legacy true</code>, but obviously that's not a good long-term solution</p>",
        "id": 608414277,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1783334218
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>(in fact in that setting, it works with or without <code> (dependent := true)</code>, and even with  <code>(dependent := false)</code>)</p>",
        "id": 608414468,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1783334289
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>We deliberately decided that the result type of a <code>match</code> in <code>do</code> notation is no longer refined. <code>(dependent := true)</code> is mostly meant as a workaround for some of the previous use cases; it is not intended as a full-fledged feature. The reason for that is that the type of join points would need to partially refined (in the arg type but not in the result type). It's hard to implement, incomplete and also inefficient. The signalling is: <code>do</code> notation should scale to large, non-dependent programs at the cost of a few dependent ones.</p>\n<p>I'll look at whether there is an obvious bug in what <code>(dependent := true)</code> is doing, but if there's no clear and easy fix I'm afraid there is no other way than to remove that use of <code>do</code> from your code.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>(in fact in that setting, it works with or without  <code>(dependent := true)</code>, and even with  <code>(dependent := false)</code>)</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Yes, the old do elaborator simply ignores that flag.</p>",
        "id": 608485115,
        "sender_full_name": "Sebastian Graf",
        "timestamp": 1783353673
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>It sounds like if dependent types are not allowed, <code>dependent := true</code> should emit an error saying that this is not supported?</p>",
        "id": 608487888,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1783354558
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Turns out that it indeed was a bug that was easy to fix: <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover/lean4/pull/14297\">lean4#14297</a>. Thanks for reporting it!</p>",
        "id": 608639937,
        "sender_full_name": "Sebastian Graf",
        "timestamp": 1783407507
    }
]